ho  were born during our tenure there  baptized this church loved children  welcoming them with open arms you can  imagine then that going back some 25  years after I had left that place was a  trip down memory night Lane and of  course the first thing I noticed were  all the things that had changed the big  old stove that used to sit right up  front near the pulpit in that church and  would draw people toward the front on  cold winter days it's no longer do there  they have central heat now the church  now boasts two red brick buildings on  its property one that looks just like  the little toy churches you see on  Christmas cards covered in snow red  bricks green pointed roof that's the old  sanctuary the other now an educational  building and the tombstones in the  cemetery beside the church now bear the  names of many saints I knew including  all three of the dairy farmers who were  elders there when I served at church but  some things are still the same and one  of them is that the little white house  right across the street from the church  where the widow in today's Gospel  reading lives is still there I say she  is the widow in today's Gospel reading  because I never hear that text from arc  read that I don't think about Ali reins  Ali reigns was well into her 80s when I  first became her pastor and her life had  been hard married for years to a man who  was physically abusive and now died she  had the sole care for her to grown adult  sons were both mentally and physically  disabled she had very little in terms of  this world's good living only on Social  Security and I'm sure she lived in that  little house on Sam's dairy farm  rent-free she couldn't get out to church  herself but every first Sunday of the  month like clockwork she would send one  of those sons across the street to make  her offering  often it was Jean a grown man with a  childlike love of tractors who would  drag his bad foot down the aisle with  Ali reins offering envelope in hand to  place her a few dollars most of what she  had to live on in the offering plate  after church on communion Sundays I  would always go see mrs. rain taking her  communion and she never let me go  without also feeding me she would take  me back to the kitchen where she had a  bit of baked chicken some green beans  and neighbor had given and always sweet  tea a southern staple and she would  insist that I eat I hated to eat her  food because I knew how little she had  but what I learned from Ali reigns was  when you have little there is great joy  in giving and that was one thing she  could still do in the Gospel reading  today from work Jesus encounters a widow  who loved to give as much as Ali rains  she didn't have much either only two  copper coins which are equal to about a  penny and yet she clearly had great  faith who knows what hardship she had  endured in her life who knows how she  survived in that day since her husband  the sole source of support for her had  died who knows how she got along in that  very patriarchal culture where women had  few rights but she like Ali rains loved  God and love to give and so in this  great act of devotion she goes to the  temple in jerusalem and goes up to one  of those thirteen trumpet-shaped vessels  where they would collect the offerings  of pilgrims coming to worship there and  she puts her two coins in can you hear  them singing down the walls until they  drop at the bottom I'm guessing she  alone could hear it but Jesus saw her  not only does he see her he lifts her up  as a model of faith and faithfulness for  his disciple  see that woman that widow he says to  them she has given more than anybody  else in this place put together they out  of their abundance gay but she she has  given her all I think it's helpful to  remember what Jesus had been doing when  you encountered this widow for he had  already had quite a long and trying day  I'm guessing he had spent the first part  of that day in the court of the  Israelites where only Jewish men were  allowed to go listening and answering  the questions of the scribes and man did  they have questions for him questions  about the nature of his authority  questions about the nature of the  resurrection questions about whether or  not they should pay taxes to Caesar  questions about which was the first and  greatest commandment questions about how  the Messiah can be David's son it was at  the end of this long and grueling period  of questioning that Jesus invites his  disciples to go with him down the 12  steps that separate the court of the  Israelites from the court of the women  and to go to that place where the women  and the lepers and others considered  unclean in that society went for worship  and it was there in that lower court  that Jesus notices the widow and lifts  her up as an example for us to emulate  the church through the years has loved  this story and especially likes to pull  it out this time of year which in many  churches is stewardship season and to  encourage all of us to give as  sacrificially as the widow did but you  know what's remarkable to me about this  story is how Jesus sees her picture who  was in the temple that day the priests  making their burnt offerings on the  altars the scribes asking their  questions and running around in their  long robes all sorts of other  worshippers putting their bigger coins  that lined down that jar in the temple  treasury the disciples worried  Jesus answers to the scribe questions  are going to get him an even more  trouble and somehow in the midst of all  that Jesus sees this Widow sees her  faith sees her faithfulness and lifts  her up for us all I don't know about you  but I sometimes find it hard to find the  widows in our society or to see them  because they don't draw a lot of  attention to themselves rather than  hanging out with us better educated  types they're often behind the scenes  somewhere sweeping the floor emptying  the trash cooking our meals but  eventually I have come to see that  they're always present at the first  seminary where I taught the widow's name  was Irene Jenkins once a week regular as  clockwork Irene would come on knock on  the door to my office headphones clearly  attached to her ears dragging a vacuum  cleaner behind her asking if she could  interrupt me long enough to back in the  floor and empty the trash for a long  time I didn't see I rain jinkins because  my head was buried in books and class  preparations but then one day is she  hummed her way around my office  vacuuming I looked up and I said Irene  tell me about yourself and that's when I  heard about her very hard life about the  husband who'd left her the two boys she  still cared for her worries about one of  them and whether he could stay out of  prison this time and then she started  talking about her deep Pentecostal faith  and how good the Lord had been to her at  her life and what miracle she had  witnessed and I realized that Irene like  the widow laughs to give and a part of  how she gave was keeping those seminary  buildings as clean as possible to  prepare people for ministry in the  church at the last church where i served  Fifth Avenue Presbyterian Church in New  York City one of the big Avenue churches  the widow's name was Laura  Laura was not immediately noticed by me  either because she didn't serve on the  officers boards and she didn't come to a  whole lot of church functions but one  Sunday after I preached a sermon on God  and money Laura came up and asked if she  could come talk to me that week that's  when I found out that for Laura money  was a constant struggle she was mentally  disabled could not hold a job and lived  in constant fear that she was going to  be kicked out of her apartment and have  to live on the streets but oh my did she  love God and did she love to give  Reverend Nora she would say i'd like to  send you some of my artwork via email  this week to brighten up your day if you  wouldn't mind and i would get some of  laura's quirky artwork and it would make  me smile or at the end of a conversation  about her life she'd say Reverend nor if  you don't mind I'd like to pray for you  too and then Laura would launch into  some of the most beautiful and eloquent  and heartfelt and honest prayers with  God I have ever heard you see I was  battling cancer that year and her gifts  of art and prayer to me where is  precious as the widow's mite it can be  hard to see the widow here at Yale  faculty get noticed a lot administrators  get noticed most students get noticed  but what about the people who clinger  clean our buildings who cook and serve  our meals who keep our grounds looking  beautiful what about the people who  bagged our groceries at the store the  work on our cars who serve our food at  restaurants do we really see them like  Jesus saw them my daughter Leah Nora is  one of those Saints in life that my  husband and I say we want to be more  like when we grow up she lives the faith  we preach and she's great at seeing  people whenever I go to a restaurant  with leanora she immediately notices the  people serving her food and says hey how  are you how's your day going  if she walks down the hall at the  divinity schools with me and some  buddies mopping the floor she stops to  thank them for their work and one time  when I was with her and a person  approached her and said can you spare me  some change she reached deep in her  pocket pulled out a dollar she keeps for  just that purpose handed it to the man  and then said what's your name and  what's your story tell me about you and  after listening for a long long time she  said you know Mom I think there are a  lot of people around we just want  somebody to know their name and listen  to their story she's right I think but  that truth not only applies to our  personalized it applies to how we see  our nation's policies to who do we see  whose stories do we seek out when we're  making our political decisions do we see  the widow are only those who talk longer  and louder and are more prestigious than  she sometimes I fear that this whole  debate over health care reform in this  nation depends a lot on where we're  sitting when we think about such issues  are we sitting in the upper court of the  temple where those who have access to  health care sit in their long robes and  chat about what it's going to cost us or  are we sitting in the court of the women  for the people with chronic disease and  the single parents of households are  struggling to make ends meet and do not  have health care the things I love the  most about that little country church I  saw served was that it sees people like  Jesus sees them they saw those in the  broader community in need of a church  home and reached out they saw children  and welcome them into their midst they  saw the poor and cared for them but  that's not all they saw they also saw  those who were getting the raw end of a  justice system in the late 1960s the  State Corporation Commission of Virginia  came to that rural area of Virginia and  condemned a lot of  and that lay around a lake because it  wanted to build a cooling pool for its  nuclear power plant the elders in that  little church dairy farmers all were  understandably deeply disturbed by this  and they took the State Corporation  Commission to court but not just on  their own behalf they went around that  neighborhood and found every single  person who was living on a tiny plot of  land who was going to be affected and  who could not afford a lawyer and put  them on their lawsuit so that they too  could get justice who do you see in your  daily life on this campus in the city in  this community and who do you fail to  see by his example Jesus invites all of  us to go to those places we might not  ordinarily sit so that we can see the  widows and if we will see them truly see  them like Jesus did I have a feeling it  will change our lives and maybe it will  also change our nation's policies for  those who have given so generously to  God and to others don't they deserve  some generosity as well amen

